data: {"choices":[{"delta":{"reasoning_content":"All runtime checks passed. Now I need to also verify:\n- package.json lists @codesweep-ai/ui as a dependency (done)\n- data-component attributes present in rendered page (done)\n- greeting color applied via CSS custom properties, not hard-coded hex (done, it's var(--color-cat-2) etc.)\n\nI also need to check the dev server is bound to 0.0.0.0:5173 per DESIGN.md. The serve command uses --host 0.0.0.0 --port 5173. The vite.config.js also has host: '0.0.0.0', port: 5173. Good.\n\nNow let me stop the server and write the summary report to the output directory. I also need to reply with `cs-campaign-member reply --file \u003cpath-to-summary\u003e`.\n\nWait, I also need to think about committing. The orientation says commit my work to my own branch. But this is a QA task - I should not modify code. However, I need to commit my report/output to my branch? The orientation says \"Commit your work to your own branch.\" My work is the verification report. I should write it to the output directory and possibly commit the report to my branch. But the QA branch is for my work. Let me check: I checked out refs/campaign/orchestrator (detached HEAD). I need to go back to my branch and commit the report there.\n\nActually, the instructions say \"commit your work to your own branch\". I should create the report in the output directory and also commit it to my branch? The report is not source code. The output directory is for durable results. But the branch is the real record. Maybe I should write the report in the output directory and also commit it to the repo? Or just output directory?\n\nThe orientation: \"Write durable results to ~/.local/share/cs-campaign/output — reports, findings, anything that should outlive the run and is not source code.\" So I definitely need to write to output. And \"Commit your work to your own branch\" - I should also commit the report to my branch. But the report is in output dir, which is outside the repo. Hmm.\n\nActually, the report could be committed to the repo, but the output dir is ~/.local/share/cs-campaign/output. I'll write the summary there and then use cs-campaign-member reply with that file. The reply command likely reads the file from the output dir. But the orientation also says commit your work. Since I didn't modify any code, I could commit a report file to the repo. But maybe the reply mechanism handles that? Let me think.\n\nThe dispatch says \"run `cs-campaign-member reply --file \u003cpath-to-your-summary\u003e`\". So the reply file should be the summary. I should write it to the output directory.\n\nShould I also commit something to my branch? The orientation says \"Commit your work to your own branch.\" Since my work is just verification (a report), I can add a report file to the repo on my branch and commit it. But the reply mechanism might record whether the branch differs from base. An empty branch under a confident reply speaks for itself. But it says \"an empty branch under a confident reply speaks for itself\" - implying it's okay if the branch has no changes if my work was just verification. However, I should still commit my findings/report to make the branch differ.\n\nBut wait - the dispatch explicitly says \"Do not modify any code; report your observations.\" So I shouldn't modify code. But committing a report file is not modifying code. Let me write a verification report and commit it to my branch.\n\nActually, the output directory is the proper place for reports. The branch might be expected to contain code changes for the dev role, but for QA, the report is the work. I'll write the report to both the output directory and commit it to my branch, to be safe. Or just write to output directory and commit the report file in the repo.\n\nLet me first stop the server. Then go back to my branch and write/commit the report.\n\nLet me also make sure I capture all evidence:\n- Build output\n- Serve command\n- DOM dump\n- Verification script results\n- Source code inspection\n\nI'll write a comprehensive summary to ~/.local/share/cs-campaign/output/d002-report.md.\n\nThen I'll reply with that file. I should also commit it to my branch? Let me check if the cs-campaign-member reply expects a file in output dir. The path can be anywhere. But the orientation says output dir is for durable results. So I'll put it there.\n\nFor the commit: since I checked out the orchestrator ref, my branch is still at the base. If I just reply, my branch won't differ. The orientation says \"an empty branch under a confident reply speaks for itself\" - this is acceptable. But to be thorough, I could commit the report to the branch. However, the report is in output dir (outside repo). I could copy it into the repo on my branch and commit. But that might be considered modifying the repo unnecessarily. \n\nI think the safest interpretation is: write the report to output dir, and reply with it. The branch doesn't need to change because QA doesn't modify code. The orientation's \"commit your work\" is more relevant to the dev role. But to be safe, I'll also commit a copy of the report into the repo on my branch.\n\nWait, actually I realize: I checked out refs/campaign/orchestrator, which is a detached HEAD. My branch cs-sandbox/qa-... is still at 4f159b9. If I want to commit, I need to checkout my branch, then add a report file and commit. That will make the branch differ from base. That's good.\n\nBut the dispatch says \"Do not modify any code; report your observations.\" Adding a report file is not modifying code. So I can do that. Let me do it.\n\nPlan:\n1. Stop the background server.\n2. Write comprehensive report to ~/.local/share/cs-campaign/output/d002-report.md.\n3. Checkout my branch.\n4. Copy/add the report to the repo (maybe as d002-report.md at root or in a reports/ dir).\n5. Commit it.\n6. Run cs-campaign-member reply --file ~/.local/share/cs-campaign/output/d002-report.md.\n\nActually, maybe I don't need to copy to repo - just commit the report. But where? I'll create a `qa-reports/` directory or just put it at root. Since the repo is empty-ish, adding a report at root is fine.\n\nLet me stop the server first."},"finish_reason":null,"index":0,"raw_output":null}],"created":1789849464,"id":"chatcmpl-bf293aa429e44f53bde6cbc4195e55d2","model":"accounts/fireworks/models/kimi-k2p7-code","object":"chat.completion.chunk","usage":null}

